<section class="resource-hero"><span class="resource-kicker">PRACTICAL CAREER TOOLS</span><h1>Resume & Application Guides</h1><p>Learn how to present your education, school activities, projects, skills, and experiences clearly when applying for training, scholarships, internships, or entry-level opportunities.</p></section>
<div class="row g-3">
 <div class="col-lg-6"><article class="resource-card"><h3>1. Start with clear contact details</h3><p>Use your professional name, a working email address, and an appropriate contact number. Do not include unnecessary sensitive personal information.</p></article></div>
 <div class="col-lg-6"><article class="resource-card"><h3>2. Write a short profile</h3><p>In two or three sentences, describe your current education level, relevant strengths, interests, and the opportunity you are seeking. Be honest and specific.</p></article></div>
 <div class="col-lg-6"><article class="resource-card"><h3>3. Present your education</h3><p>List your school, grade level or program, and dates. Include relevant subjects, academic projects, awards, or training when useful.</p></article></div>
 <div class="col-lg-6"><article class="resource-card"><h3>4. Include experience beyond paid work</h3><p>School organizations, volunteer work, family responsibilities, community projects, and class activities can demonstrate useful skills. Describe your actual contribution.</p></article></div>
 <div class="col-lg-6"><article class="resource-card"><h3>5. Show skills with examples</h3><p>Instead of listing only “leadership,” explain a real activity where you helped organize a group, coordinate tasks, or solve a problem.</p></article></div>
 <div class="col-lg-6"><article class="resource-card"><h3>6. Tailor each application</h3><p>Read the opportunity carefully and highlight the most relevant education, projects, and skills. Follow the requested format and file type.</p></article></div>
</div>
<h2 class="resource-section-title">Simple student resume outline</h2>
<div class="content-card"><ol class="resource-list"><li><strong>Full name and contact information</strong></li><li><strong>Career or application objective</strong> — a short, opportunity-specific statement.</li><li><strong>Education</strong> — school, grade/program, and relevant learning.</li><li><strong>Projects and activities</strong> — school projects, clubs, volunteer work, and responsibilities.</li><li><strong>Skills</strong> — technical, digital, communication, and practical skills you can demonstrate.</li><li><strong>Training, awards, or certificates</strong> — include dates and issuing organizations when applicable.</li><li><strong>References</strong> — provide only when requested and with the person's permission.</li></ol></div>
<h2 class="resource-section-title">Before you submit</h2>
<div class="content-card"><ul class="resource-list"><li>Check spelling, grammar, dates, and contact details.</li><li>Use a clean, readable layout and consistent formatting.</li><li>Keep information truthful; never invent experience or qualifications.</li><li>Save a PDF copy unless the application asks for another format.</li><li>Use a sensible filename, such as <em>Firstname_Lastname_Resume.pdf</em>.</li><li>Ask a teacher, guardian, or guidance counselor to review it.</li><li>Submit only through verified application channels. Be cautious of requests for money or sensitive information from unverified recruiters.</li></ul></div>
